---
/* Layout for DR4W blog posts (Transmissions) */

import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../components/TableOfContents.astro';
import SectionComponents from '../components/SectionComponents.astro';
import Diagrams from '../components/Diagrams.astro';
import InteractiveWidgets from '../components/InteractiveWidgets.astro';
import ArticleFooter from '../components/ArticleFooter.astro';
import FadingBackgroundImages from '../components/FadingBackgroundImages.astro';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  subtitle: string;
  author: string;
  date: Date;
  readingTime: number;
}

const { title, subtitle, author, date, readingTime } = Astro.props;

const parts = [
  { number: 1, title: 'What is an LLM?', id: 'part-1' },
  { number: 2, title: 'Tokenization', id: 'part-2' },
  { number: 3, title: 'Embeddings & vectors', id: 'part-3' },
  { number: 4, title: 'Transformers & attention', id: 'part-4' },
  { number: 5, title: 'Inference, context, prompting', id: 'part-5' },
  { number: 6, title: 'What is an agent?', id: 'part-6' },
  { number: 7, title: 'What is a harness?', id: 'part-7' },
  { number: 8, title: 'The tooling we use', id: 'part-8' }
];
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>{title} — SIGNAL LOG // dr4w</title>
  <meta name="description" content={subtitle} />
  <meta name="author" content={author} />
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0A0A0F" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
  
  <!-- Fonts -->
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  
  <style>
    @import '../styles/global.css';
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <!-- Fading background images -->
  <FadingBackgroundImages />

  <main class="main-container">
    <!-- Hero section -->
    <Hero title={title} subtitle={subtitle} readingTime={readingTime} />

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <TableOfContents parts={parts} />

    <!-- Main article -->
    <article class="transmission-article">
        <!-- Part 1: What is an LLM? -->
        <section id="part-1" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 01</div>
            <h2>What is an LLM?</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll know what this "AI" we chat with really is, what it does under the hood, and what it is <strong>not</strong>.</p>
          </header>
          
          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p>An <strong>LLM</strong> (Large Language Model) is, at its core, a <strong>text-completion machine</strong>. You give it a beginning, and it guesses the next small piece of text. Then it looks at everything again (including what it just wrote) and guesses the next piece. And again, until the answer is complete.</p>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> it's your phone keyboard's <strong>autocomplete</strong>, turbocharged. The keyboard suggests the next word; an LLM suggests the next word so well that it can write an entire email, a piece of code, or a coherent summary.</p>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <Diagrams type="D1" title="Autocomplete loop" />
          
          <p>What's impressive isn't the idea (predicting the next word). It's the <strong>scale</strong>. The model read an enormous amount of text and got so good at predicting that, in order to get the next word right, it effectively "learned" grammar, facts, style, and even patterns of reasoning.</p>
          
          <h3>How it "learned" all that (very roughly)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The model was shown <strong>a huge amount of text</strong> (books, websites, code) and asked, millions upon millions of times: "given this sentence, what's the next word?"</li>
            <li>Every time it got it wrong, its <strong>internal numbers</strong> (the <em>weights</em>) were nudged a tiny bit so it would miss less next time. That's <strong>training</strong>.</li>
            <li>At the end, those weights are "frozen" into one giant file. <strong>That file is the model.</strong> It doesn't learn anything on its own after that.</li>
          </ul>
          
          <Diagrams type="D2" title="Three-phase timeline" />
          
          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="What an LLM is NOT">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>It's not a database.</strong> It doesn't "look up" the answer in a table; it <strong>generates</strong> it word by word. That's why it sometimes makes things up (more on <em>hallucination</em> in Part 5).</li>
              <li><strong>It's not deterministic by default.</strong> The same question can produce different answers, because there's an element of random sampling when choosing the next word.</li>
              <li><strong>It doesn't "think" or "understand" like a person.</strong> It models <strong>patterns of language</strong> extremely well. That's useful and powerful, but it's different from consciousness or real comprehension.</li>
              <li><strong>It doesn't know what happened after training.</strong> If training ended last year, it knows nothing about yesterday, unless you <strong>put that information in the prompt</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <SectionComponents type="file" title="The three phases that turn raw text into an assistant">
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Pre-training.</strong> The model reads an ocean of text, just predicting the next word. It comes out knowing language and a lot about the world, but "raw": it doesn't know how to follow instructions.</li>
              <li><strong>Instruction fine-tuning.</strong> It's shown examples of "request → good answer" so it learns to <strong>follow instructions</strong> (summarize, answer, format).</li>
              <li><strong>Alignment (RLHF).</strong> Humans compare answers and say which is better; the model is adjusted to prefer helpful, safe answers and to refuse dangerous ones. This is what makes the assistant feel "polite and helpful."</li>
            </ol>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-2">Next: Tokenization</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <!-- Part 2: Tokenization -->
        <section id="part-2" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 02</div>
            <h2>Tokenization</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll understand what the model really "sees" when you type something, why that explains odd behaviors, and why it <strong>costs money</strong>.</p>
          </header>
          
          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p>The model doesn't read letters or words the way we do. Before anything else, text is chopped into <strong>tokens</strong>: small pieces that can be a whole word, part of a word, a space, or a punctuation mark. This process is called <strong>tokenization</strong>.</p>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> imagine building sentences from prefabricated <strong>LEGO bricks</strong>. You don't have one brick for every possible English word; you have a fixed set of bricks (some big, like "house"; others small, like "un" or "tion") and you build words by snapping bricks together. The model works with these "text bricks": tokens.</p>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <Diagrams type="D3" title="Tokenizer strip" />
          
          <h3>A token is not a word</h3>
          <p>A rule of thumb (for English) is that <strong>1 token ≈ 4 characters ≈ ¾ of a word</strong>. But it varies a lot with the language and the words:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Common words are usually <strong>1 token</strong> ("house", "the").</li>
            <li>Long or rare words are split into <strong>several tokens</strong> ("unconstitutionally" might become 4–5 pieces).</li>
            <li>Spaces and punctuation also count as tokens (or are part of them).</li>
            <li><strong>Languages other than English usually take more tokens</strong> to say the same thing.</li>
          </ul>
          
          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Why AI gets 'how many R's in strawberry' wrong">
            <p>Because it <strong>doesn't see letters</strong>. It sees tokens. If "strawberry" reaches the model as one token (a single number), the information about <em>which letters make it up</em> simply isn't available in an accessible way. So it <strong>guesses</strong> from patterns, and it can be wrong.</p>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <h3>Why this matters day to day</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Cost.</strong> Almost every provider charges <strong>per token</strong> (input and output). A giant prompt means more tokens, which means more money.</li>
            <li><strong>Context limit.</strong> The model's "working memory" is measured in <strong>tokens</strong>, not pages. A document may simply not fit.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed.</strong> The model generates <strong>one token at a time</strong>. More tokens in the answer means a longer wait.</li>
          </ul>
          
          <SectionComponents type="test" title="In practice">
            <p>When you use an AI coding tool, your message + open files + history + the project's rules together form the context window for that inference. Context engineering—putting only what's needed, in the right place—is a discipline of its own.</p>
          </SectionComponents>
          
          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-3">Next: Embeddings & vectors</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <!-- Part 3: Embeddings & vectors -->
        <section id="part-3" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 03</div>
            <h2>Embeddings & vectors</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll understand how a computer can measure that "car" and "automobile" mean nearly the same thing, the idea behind smart search, memory, and the famous RAG.</p>
          </header>

          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p>An <strong>embedding</strong> is a way of turning a piece of text (a word, a sentence, a paragraph) into a <strong>list of numbers</strong> that represents its <strong>meaning</strong>. Texts with similar meanings become <strong>similar</strong> lists of numbers.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> think of a <strong>map</strong>. Every city has coordinates (latitude, longitude), and nearby cities have nearby coordinates. An embedding does this for <strong>meanings</strong>: it gives each text a "coordinate of meaning." "Dog" and "puppy" land practically at the same spot; "dog" and "refrigerator" land far apart.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <Diagrams type="D4" title="Semantic space" />

          <h3>What it's for</h3>
          <p>Once meaning becomes a "position on a map," a computer can do things exact-word search can't:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Search by meaning.</strong> You search "how to reset my password" and find a document about "credential recovery," without a single word in common.</li>
            <li><strong>Group similar topics.</strong> Cluster complaints about the same issue.</li>
            <li><strong>Agent memory.</strong> Store facts, then find the relevant ones by proximity.</li>
            <li><strong>RAG.</strong> The heart of the technique that "pastes documents into the prompt" (below).</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>How "near" is measured</h3>
          <p>You compare the <strong>direction</strong> of the two lists of numbers. If two texts "point the same way" on the map of meaning, they're considered close. You'll see the name for this: <strong>cosine similarity</strong>. You don't need the formula. The intuition is "same direction = same topic."</p>

          <h3>How RAG uses embeddings</h3>
          <p><strong>RAG</strong> (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) solves a problem from Part 1: the model doesn't know <strong>your</strong> documents. The trick:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Your documents are split into chunks, and an embedding is computed for each chunk.</li>
            <li>Those embeddings are stored in a <strong>vector database</strong> (an index of "coordinates").</li>
            <li>When you ask something, the embedding of your <strong>question</strong> is computed, and the <strong>closest</strong> chunks are retrieved.</li>
            <li>Those chunks are <strong>pasted into the prompt</strong>, and the model answers based on them.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>That way the model "knows" things it never saw in training, because you handed it the right passages at the right time.</p>

          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Common misconceptions">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"An embedding is the same as a token."</strong> No. A token is a piece of text (Part 2); an embedding is a <strong>representation of meaning</strong> (a list of numbers per text).</li>
              <li><strong>"If the words are different, the texts are different."</strong> No: embeddings capture meaning, so synonyms end up close even without shared words.</li>
              <li><strong>"Vector search always finds the right thing."</strong> If the right chunk wasn't indexed, or the text was split badly, it returns junk, and the model answers confidently about junk.</li>
              <li><strong>"I can swap the embedding model whenever I like."</strong> Swapping <strong>changes the entire map</strong>, so every embedding must be recomputed (re-indexed). It's not a light update.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-4">Next: Transformers & attention</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <section id="part-4" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 04</div>
            <h2>Transformers & attention</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll understand, without math, the machinery inside every modern LLM, the <strong>transformer</strong>, and the idea that makes it work: <strong>attention</strong>.</p>
          </header>

          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p><strong>Transformer</strong> is the name of the <strong>architecture</strong> (the kind of machinery) used in practically every LLM today: GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama. It was introduced in 2017 in a paper with a title that became an in-joke in the field: <em>"Attention Is All You Need."</em></p>
            <p>The big insight is <strong>attention</strong>: when deciding the next word, the model <strong>looks at all the previous words at once</strong> and decides, for each one, <strong>how much it matters</strong> right now.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> you're reading the sentence <em>"I took the dog to the vet because <strong>he</strong> was sick."</em> To know who "he" is, your brain <strong>pays attention</strong> to "dog" back there and ignores "vet." A transformer's attention does exactly that: it weighs which earlier words are relevant for understanding or generating the next one.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <h3>Why it was revolutionary</h3>
          <p>Before transformers, models read text <strong>word by word, in order</strong>, like reading through a little hole that slides left to right. That had two problems:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>It was <strong>slow</strong> (one word at a time, with no way to take advantage of parallel processing).</li>
            <li>It <strong>forgot the beginning</strong> of long texts (information from the start got lost along the way).</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The transformer looks at the <strong>whole text at once</strong> and links any word to any other directly, however far apart they are. That made it possible to train <strong>much bigger</strong> models <strong>much faster</strong>, which is why LLMs made the leap they did.</p>

          <Diagrams type="D11" title="Multi-head attention layers" />

          <h3>How an answer gets built</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Your text is chopped into <strong>tokens</strong> (Part 2), and each token becomes a list of numbers carrying meaning + position (a relative of the <strong>embedding</strong> from Part 3).</li>
            <li>Those numbers pass through <strong>many stacked layers of attention</strong>. In each layer the model recombines the information: every token "looks at" the others and updates what it represents, given the context.</li>
            <li>At the top, the model produces a <strong>list of probabilities</strong>: for <strong>every</strong> possible token in the vocabulary, the chance it's the next one.</li>
            <li>A token is chosen from that list (Part 5), <strong>appended</strong> to the text, and everything starts over from step 1 for the <strong>next</strong> token.</li>
          </ol>

          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Common misconceptions">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"Transformer is the name of the model."</strong> No, it's the name of the <strong>architecture</strong>. GPT and Claude <em>are</em> transformers, the way a Beetle and a Civic <em>are</em> cars.</li>
              <li><strong>"Attention means it truly understands."</strong> Attention is a mathematical mechanism for "weighing relevance between tokens," not conscious comprehension.</li>
              <li><strong>"It writes the whole answer at once."</strong> No: token by token, one at a time.</li>
              <li><strong>"It reads left to right like we do."</strong> It processes all the tokens in the context in parallel; order enters as extra information, not as a "cursor" that moves along.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-5">Next: Inference, context, prompting</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <section id="part-5" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 05</div>
            <h2>Inference, context & prompting</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll understand what happens <strong>when you hit enter</strong>: how the model uses its "working memory," why the same question can give different answers, how to write good requests, and why AI sometimes <strong>makes things up</strong>.</p>
          </header>

          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p><strong>Inference</strong> is the fancy word for "<strong>using</strong> the model to generate an answer," as opposed to <em>training</em> it, which happened long ago. Every time you talk to an AI, you're running an inference.</p>
            <p>To answer, the model takes everything in the <strong>context window</strong> and generates the response <strong>one token at a time</strong> (Part 4) until it decides it's finished.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <h3>The context window is a whiteboard</h3>
          <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> imagine a <strong>whiteboard</strong> of fixed size. On it fit: your instructions, your question, the conversation history, and any document you pasted. The model only "knows" what's written <strong>on that board right now</strong>.</p>
          <p>Three important consequences:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>It's finite.</strong> If it fills up, something has to go (the start of the conversation disappears, or something doesn't fit). Size is measured in <strong>tokens</strong> (Part 2), not pages.</li>
            <li><strong>It doesn't persist on its own.</strong> Close the conversation and the board is wiped. "Remembering" across conversations requires a tool that <strong>stores and rewrites</strong> the history onto the board.</li>
            <li><strong>Filling it with junk makes things worse.</strong> Pasting irrelevant material doesn't just cost more, it <strong>hurts</strong>: the model gets distracted and uses what's in the middle poorly.</li>
          </ul>

          <Diagrams type="D5" title="Context window assembly" />

          <h3>Why the same question gives different answers</h3>
          <p>When the model builds its list of "likely next tokens" (Part 4), it doesn't <strong>always</strong> pick the most likely one. It does a <strong>weighted draw</strong>. A control called <strong>temperature</strong> sets how "bold" that draw is:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Low temperature</strong> → almost always picks the most likely token → more <strong>consistent, predictable</strong> answers (good for factual tasks and code).</li>
            <li><strong>High temperature</strong> → takes more risks → more <strong>varied, creative</strong> answers (good for brainstorming and prose).</li>
          </ul>

          <Diagrams type="D9" title="Temperature control" />

          <h3>Prompting: how to ask well</h3>
          <p>A <strong>prompt</strong> is the text you send. Prompting is the art of asking in a way that makes the model more likely to get it right. The basics that already solve 80% of cases:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Give context and a goal.</strong> "You are a code reviewer. Point out security bugs in this snippet:" works better than "look at this."</li>
            <li><strong>Be specific about format.</strong> "Answer in 3 bullets" or "return only the JSON."</li>
            <li><strong>Show an example</strong> of what you want (called <em>few-shot</em>): one or two "input → output" examples guide a lot.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask for step-by-step</strong> on reasoning problems ("think step by step"). It often improves the answer.</li>
            <li><strong>Separate instruction from data.</strong> Make clear what is your order and what is material to be processed.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Hallucination: why AI makes things up</h3>
          <p><strong>Hallucination</strong> is when the model answers something <strong>wrong that looks right</strong>. Remember Part 1: it <strong>generates the most plausible text</strong>; it doesn't consult a truth. When it doesn't "know," it doesn't go silent. It fills in with whatever <strong>sounds</strong> most likely, and that can be false.</p>
          <p>How to reduce it (not eliminate it):</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Put the source in the prompt</strong> (RAG, Part 3): answer based on real passages you provided.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask it to cite</strong> where it got something, and be suspicious when there's no source.</li>
            <li><strong>Lower the temperature</strong> for factual tasks.</li>
            <li><strong>Verify</strong> anything critical. In high-stakes or regulated settings this is mandatory.</li>
          </ul>

          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Common misconceptions">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"It looked that up on the internet."</strong> Only if a search tool is plugged in. Without one, it generates from memory, with knowledge frozen at training time.</li>
              <li><strong>"If it answered confidently, it's right."</strong> Confidence in the text is <strong>not</strong> evidence of truth. Hallucinations come across confident.</li>
              <li><strong>"It failed once, so the model is bad."</strong> Because of randomness, try again and/or improve the prompt before concluding.</li>
              <li><strong>"The more I paste into the prompt, the better."</strong> Usually it gets <strong>worse</strong>: more cost and more distraction. Include <strong>only what's needed</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="test" title="In practice">
            <p>When you use an AI coding tool, your message + open files + history + the project's rules together form the context window for that inference. When an agent "forgets" something said at the start of a long session, it's almost always because the context window <strong>filled up</strong>, not ill will. <strong>Context engineering</strong> (putting only what's needed, in the right place) is a discipline of its own.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-6">Next: What is an agent?</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <section id="part-6" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 06</div>
            <h2>What is an agent?</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll understand how AI goes from "just answering text" to <strong>doing things</strong> (opening files, running commands, querying systems) and why that changes everything, including the risks.</p>
          </header>

          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p>Until now, AI was a "genie in a box": you ask, it answers in text, and that's the end. An <strong>agent</strong> takes the genie out of the box: it's an LLM placed inside a <strong>loop</strong> where it can <strong>act</strong> on the world and <strong>see the result</strong> of its action before continuing.</p>
            <p>The loop, in four steps, repeats until the task is done:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Goal.</strong> It receives an objective ("fix the login bug").</li>
              <li><strong>Reason.</strong> It decides the next step.</li>
              <li><strong>Act.</strong> It uses a <strong>tool</strong> (read a file, run a test, search the web).</li>
              <li><strong>Observe.</strong> It reads the tool's result and goes back to step 2.</li>
            </ol>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> the difference between <strong>asking for a recipe</strong> (the model gives you the text) and <strong>having a cook in the kitchen</strong> (the agent opens the fridge, tastes, adjusts the salt, and serves). The cook doesn't get everything right on the first try: they <strong>act, taste, and correct</strong> in a cycle.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <h3>What "tools" are</h3>
          <p>On its own, the model only produces text. A <strong>tool</strong> is a function someone has made available for the agent to use: "read file X," "run this command," "look up the customer's balance." The model doesn't run the tool. It <strong>asks</strong> for it to be run, and the program around it (the <em>harness</em>, Part 7) runs it and hands back the result.</p>
          <p>Every tool is a <strong>door to the real world</strong>. That's what gives the agent power, and also what demands care: a tool that deletes files is useful and dangerous at the same time.</p>

          <h3>When the agent stops</h3>
          <p>An agent with no brakes can spin in circles and <strong>burn time and money</strong> (each turn of the loop is new inferences, so more tokens). That's why every serious agent has stopping criteria:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Goal reached</strong> (the test passed, the file was fixed).</li>
            <li><strong>A cap on steps/iterations</strong> (so it doesn't run forever).</li>
            <li><strong>A budget</strong> of tokens/time.</li>
            <li><strong>Asking the human for help</strong> when it gets stuck or when the action is risky.</li>
          </ul>

          <Diagrams type="D12" title="Agent loop: goal → reason → act → observe" />

          <h3>In practice</h3>
          <p>AI coding assistants are agents. When you say "fix this test," they read files, edit code, and run commands in a loop, exactly the cycle above. The tools they use include reading/editing files, running terminal commands, and calling <strong>MCP servers</strong> (Part 8). The <strong>approval prompt</strong> before a risky command is the loop's "ask the human for help," which doubles as a safety control.</p>

          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Common misconceptions">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"An agent is a different/smarter model."</strong> No: it's the <strong>same kind of model</strong> placed in a loop with tools. The ability to "act" comes from the structure around it.</li>
              <li><strong>"The model runs the commands."</strong> It <strong>asks</strong>; the harness executes. That separation is what lets the system ask for your approval before anything dangerous.</li>
              <li><strong>"More agents = better."</strong> Multiple agents bring parallelism, but also cost and coordination. Start with <strong>one</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>"If it has tools, it never hallucinates."</strong> It can still <strong>choose the wrong tool</strong> or misread the result. Tools reduce errors; they don't eliminate them.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-7">Next: What is a harness?</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <section id="part-7" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 07</div>
            <h2>What is a harness?</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll understand what a <strong>harness</strong> is, why the model alone does nothing useful, and what tools like <strong>Cascade</strong> and <strong>Devin</strong> really are.</p>
          </header>

          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p>The model (Part 1) is just a "brain" that completes text. It does <strong>not</strong> open files, run commands, remember your project, or run the agent loop (Part 6). All of that is done by the <strong>harness</strong>: the program that <strong>wraps</strong> the model and turns it into a working tool.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> the model is the <strong>engine</strong>; the harness is the <strong>whole car</strong> around it: steering wheel, pedals, dashboard, wheels. A brilliant engine sitting on the garage floor takes you nowhere. The harness is what lets you <strong>drive</strong> the model.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <p>What a coding harness (like Cascade or Devin) does for you:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Reads your project:</strong> files, structure, what's open in the editor.</li>
            <li><strong>Runs the agent loop:</strong> reason, act, observe, repeat (Part 6).</li>
            <li><strong>Executes the tools:</strong> edit a file, run the terminal, call an MCP server.</li>
            <li><strong>Builds the context:</strong> gathers your message + files + rules and sends it to the model.</li>
            <li><strong>Applies your configuration:</strong> <em>rules</em>, <em>skills</em>, <em>workflows</em> (Part 8).</li>
            <li><strong>Asks for approval</strong> before risky actions.</li>
          </ul>

          <Diagrams type="D13" title="Harness architecture" />

          <h3>Model, harness and provider: who's who</h3>
          <p>It's easier when you separate three things that often get mixed up:</p>
          <table>
            <tr>
              <td style="font-weight: bold;">Piece</td>
              <td style="font-weight: bold;">What it is</td>
              <td style="font-weight: bold;">Example</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Model</strong></td>
              <td>the "brain" that generates text</td>
              <td>GPT, Claude, Gemini</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Harness</strong></td>
              <td>the program that runs the agent and uses the model</td>
              <td>Cascade, Devin</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Provider</strong></td>
              <td>who hosts and serves the model via API</td>
              <td>OpenAI, Anthropic, a cloud vendor</td>
            </tr>
          </table>
          <p>The same harness can use <strong>different models</strong> underneath, and the same model can be used by <strong>different harnesses</strong>. They're <strong>independent</strong> pieces that snap together.</p>

          <h3>In practice</h3>
          <p>When you chat in <strong>Windsurf</strong>, it's <strong>Cascade</strong> assembling the context and running the tools. When you use the <strong>Desktop</strong> app or the terminal, it's <strong>Devin</strong>. The <strong>rules</strong>, <strong>skills</strong> and <strong>workflows</strong> a team maintains (next part) only take effect because <strong>the harness loads them</strong> and injects them into the model. <strong>MCP servers</strong> are tools the harness plugs into the agent.</p>

          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Common misconceptions">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"Cascade/Devin is the AI model."</strong> No: they're <strong>harnesses</strong>. The model (the brain) runs underneath and can even be swapped.</li>
              <li><strong>"The harness thinks."</strong> It does <strong>not</strong> generate text; it <strong>organizes</strong> the work and calls the model, which generates. A harness is smart plumbing, not intelligence.</li>
              <li><strong>"It doesn't matter where I put my rules/workflows."</strong> It matters a lot: each harness reads specific folders.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="next" href="#part-8">Next: The tooling we use</SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <section id="part-8" class="transmission-part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-number">LOG 08</div>
            <h2>The tooling we use</h2>
            <p class="part-intro">By the end of this part you'll recognize the pieces that show up day to day (<strong>rules, skills, workflows, MCP, spec-kit and the orchestrator</strong>) and know what each is for, without memorizing anything.</p>
          </header>

          <SectionComponents type="core" title="The core idea">
            <p>The harness (Part 7) becomes far more useful when you <strong>configure</strong> it with your team's knowledge and procedures. Instead of re-explaining everything in every conversation, you leave these things <strong>written down in standard places</strong>, and the harness loads them automatically.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <SectionComponents type="analogy">
            <p><strong>Analogy:</strong> think of an <strong>excellent new hire with no memory from one day to the next</strong>. To make them productive, you leave things in writing: the <strong>house rules</strong> (rules), a few <strong>skill manuals</strong> (skills), <strong>procedure checklists</strong> (workflows), and a <strong>keyring for system access</strong> (MCP). The tooling is exactly that kit.</p>
          </SectionComponents>

          <h2 style="margin-top: 2rem;">The pieces, one by one</h2>

          <h3>Rules: the permanent instructions</h3>
          <p>Instructions that apply to <strong>every conversation</strong>, so you don't repeat yourself: "answer in English," "never run git without approval," architecture standards, and so on.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>They live in folders like <code>.windsurf/rules/</code> and <code>.devin/rules/</code>, and in files like <code>AGENTS.md</code> at the project root.</li>
            <li>They're the practical equivalent of the <strong>system prompt</strong> from Part 5: they frame the agent's behavior.</li>
            <li>Since each harness reads from slightly different places (Part 7), the safe move is to pick a <strong>canonical location</strong> every harness you use reads.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Skills: capabilities on demand</h3>
          <p>Packages of "how to do one specific thing" that the agent <strong>pulls in when it needs them</strong> (e.g. setting up an integration, following a technical procedure). They live in folders like <code>.devin/skills/</code> or <code>.windsurf/skills/</code>. Unlike rules (which always apply), a skill is triggered <strong>when the topic comes up</strong>.</p>

          <h3>Workflows: versioned procedures (slash commands)</h3>
          <p>Well-defined step-by-step procedures that anyone (or any agent) runs the same way, invoked by a <strong>slash command</strong>. A few examples:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>/scaffold</code> creates the structure for a new feature.</li>
            <li><code>/review</code> reviews code or security.</li>
            <li><code>/adr</code> writes an architecture decision record.</li>
            <li><code>/deploy</code> validates deployment configuration.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>MCP (Model Context Protocol): the "USB port" for tools and data</h3>
          <p>A standard for plugging tools and data sources into the agent in a uniform way. Instead of every integration being a different one-off hack, MCP standardizes the connector.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>An <strong>MCP server</strong> offers <strong>tools</strong> (actions) and <strong>resources</strong> (data); the agent is the client that uses them.</li>
            <li>Every MCP server is also an <strong>access point</strong>, which makes it a security surface. Treat what you plug in with care.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>spec-kit: specification-driven development</h3>
          <p>A (CLI) tool for working <strong>"spec-first"</strong>: you write the specification and the plan first, then generate and run the tasks. It shows up as <code>/speckit.*</code> commands. It keeps you from charging into code without a clear agreement on what to build.</p>

          <h3>Orchestrators: coordinating many agents</h3>
          <p>When a task is too big for a single agent, an <strong>orchestrator</strong> coordinates several in parallel. Think of it as "many cooks in an organized kitchen."</p>

          <h2 style="margin-top: 2rem;">How it all fits together</h2>

          <Diagrams type="D16" title="System architecture" />

          <ul style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <li><strong>You</strong> give the goal. <strong>Rules/skills/workflows</strong> say <em>how</em> to act.</li>
            <li>The <strong>harness</strong> assembles everything, calls the <strong>model</strong>, and executes the <strong>tools</strong> (local/MCP).</li>
            <li>On big tasks, the <strong>orchestrator</strong> coordinates several agents.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem;">Where a beginner should start</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Read the project's rules</strong> (<code>AGENTS.md</code> and the rules folder). It's the "house manual."</li>
            <li><strong>Try a simple workflow</strong> (e.g. <code>/review</code> on a small snippet).</li>
            <li><strong>Ask the agent for small, verifiable tasks</strong> and check the result (Part 5: AI can hallucinate).</li>
            <li><strong>Don't approve commands you don't understand.</strong> Approval is your rein (Part 7).</li>
            <li>When you want the "why" behind each piece, go back and reread the earlier parts.</li>
          </ol>

          <SectionComponents type="memory" title="Common misconceptions">
            <ul>
              <li><strong>"Rules, skills and workflows are the same thing."</strong> Rules apply <strong>always</strong>; skills are <strong>on demand</strong>; workflows are <strong>procedures</strong> you trigger.</li>
              <li><strong>"I created the workflow, so everyone has it."</strong> It depends on the <strong>harness</strong> (Cascade runs it; Devin doesn't) and on the <strong>folder</strong> where you saved it (Part 7).</li>
              <li><strong>"MCP is a product/app."</strong> It's a <strong>protocol</strong> (a connector standard); the <em>servers</em> are the concrete pieces.</li>
              <li><strong>"The orchestrator is mandatory."</strong> No: most day-to-day tasks are <strong>a single agent</strong>. Orchestration is for large, parallelizable work.</li>
            </ul>
          </SectionComponents>
        </section>
        
        <!-- Closing -->
        <section class="transmission-closing">
          <h2>The full mental model</h2>
          <p>You now have the complete picture: from the token (Part 2) to the transformer (Part 4), from inference (Part 5) to the agent (Part 6), from the harness (Part 7) to the tooling (Part 8).</p>
          <p><strong>One-line version:</strong> <em>a model completes text; wrap it in a loop with tools and you get an agent; wrap that in a harness and you get something you can actually work with; configure the harness with rules, skills, workflows and MCP and it works the way your team works.</em></p>
        </section>
      </article>

